    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `build_server()` in `app/server.py` and it works: the tests pass and I called both tools over stdio.\n\n**How it's built:**\n- It uses mcp 2.2.0's high-level `MCPServer` (imported from `mcp.server`). This is the new name for `FastMCP` in this version.\n- The server is `MCPServer(\"notes\")`, and both tools are registered with `@server.tool()`.\n- `add_note` stores the body in `NOTES` and returns `saved <title>`.\n- `word_count` returns `len(body.split())`, so any run of whitespace, including newlines, counts as one separator.\n- For an unknown title, `word_count` raises `ToolError(\"no such note: <title>\")`. In this SDK version, only `ToolError` passes its message through to the model. A plain `KeyError` or `ValueError` would reach the model only as the generic `Error executing tool word_count`.\n- `python -m app.server` still calls `build_server().run()`, which defaults to stdio. No dependencies were added.\n\n**What I ran:**\n- `pytest -q`: 1 passed.\n- `python -m app.smoke` lists `add_note` and `word_count`.\n- A real stdio client run gave:\n  - `add_note(\"a\", \"one two  three\\nfour\")` returned `saved a`.\n  - `word_count(\"a\")` returned `4`.\n  - `word_count(\"zz\")` returned `is_error=True` with the text `Error executing tool word_count: no such note: zz`. The SDK adds that prefix itself, and the text still contains \"no such note\".\n\n**One thing to clean up:** I couldn't delete the check script I used, `_chk.py` in the project root, because the delete needed your approval. Please remove it or let me delete it."
  },
